From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 17:36:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: aw9523: serialize the interrupt thread's port reads

The driver's regmap sets disable_locking and relies on i2c_lock to
serialize register access. aw9523_irq_thread_func() reads both input
ports through that regmap without taking the lock. A GPIO consumer can
be reading at the same moment, for example a matrix keypad scanning in
the work its row interrupt scheduled. The two reads then share regmap's
unlocked work buffer, and one can return the other's register.

The Gemini PDA keyboard shows this: an AW9523 matrix in row interrupt
mode. While the scan read the rows (port 0) with column 5 driven, the
interrupt thread read port 1. The scan received port 1's value: 0xdf,
column 5 low, instead of 0xfb, row 2 low. So A was reported as the key in
row 5. The read at the end of the scan returned port 1 as well. It never
matched the start, so a held key kept rescheduling scans.

Take i2c_lock around the thread's port reads, as the GPIO callbacks do.
Release it before dispatching nested interrupts, whose handlers may use
the GPIO callbacks themselves.

Not yet run on hardware with this change.
---
 drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-aw9523.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-aw9523.c b/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-aw9523.c
index 9a7e7c989..c8169258b 100644
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-aw9523.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-aw9523.c
@@ -462,15 +462,20 @@ static irqreturn_t aw9523_irq_thread_func(int irq, void *dev_id)
 	unsigned long changed_gpio;
 	unsigned int tmp, port_pin, i, ret;
 
+	/* The regmap has no locking of its own; i2c_lock serializes it. */
+	mutex_lock(&awi->i2c_lock);
 	for (i = 0; i < AW9523_NUM_PORTS; i++) {
 		port_pin = i * AW9523_PINS_PER_PORT;
 		ret = regmap_read(awi->regmap,
 				  AW9523_REG_IN_STATE(port_pin),
 				  &tmp);
-		if (ret)
+		if (ret) {
+			mutex_unlock(&awi->i2c_lock);
 			return ret;
+		}
 		val |= (u8)tmp << (i * 8);
 	}
+	mutex_unlock(&awi->i2c_lock);
 
 	/* Handle GPIO input release interrupt as well */
 	changed_gpio = awi->irq->cached_gpio ^ val;
